.TH CHANNEL_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
channel_create \- create a bidirectional communication channel
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

channel_create(max_size, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a bidirectional channel with two internal circular queues for
communication between an owner thread and worker threads. Both directions
share the same capacity. Uses standard malloc/free for memory management.

This is a convenience macro for channel_create_with_mprocs() with NULL
memory management procedures.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size   Maximum number of messages each direction can hold; each
           direction is backed by its own circular queue, so the same
           SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t) bound documented for
           circular_queue_create(3) applies here
err_str    Optional error string pointer
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to newly created channel on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations are thread-safe
Channel must be destroyed with channel_destroy() when done
Direction is automatically selected based on calling thread ID
The owner thread (the one that called channel_create()) must remain
alive for as long as the channel is in use: routing compares the
calling thread's ID against the ID captured at creation time, and the
OS may reuse that ID for an unrelated, later thread once the original
owner exits, which would then be silently misrouted as the owner too
fork(2) is safe with respect to each underlying circular queue's own
internal locking: a lock held by some other thread at the instant of
the fork is never inherited by the child already locked. Neither
queue owns a thread of its own, so a channel already live and in
active use across the fork remains fully usable on both sides
afterward.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR channel_create_with_mprocs (3),
.BR channel_destroy (3)
